<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HD">Example <span data-tag="02HD">4.7.1.6</span> <span class="named">(Truncated Objects of Duskin Nerves)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a $2$-category (that is, a bicategory in which every $2$-morphism is invertible), so that the Duskin nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is an $\infty $-category (Theorem <a data-tag="00AC" href="/tag/00AC">2.3.2.1</a>). Combining Remark <a data-tag="05ED" href="/tag/05ED">4.7.1.2</a> with Example <a data-tag="01L7" href="/tag/01L7">4.6.5.12</a>, we obtain the following: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
For $n \geq 1$, every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $0$-truncated if and only, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the groupoid $\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is equivalent to a set: that is, every $1$-morphism $f: C \rightarrow X$ has trivial automorphism group. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(-1)$-truncated if and only if, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the groupoid $\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is equivalent to a set having at most one element. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For $n \leq -2$, an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $n$-truncated if and only if, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the groupoid $\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is equivalent to a set having exactly one element. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
